1981, biologist Rupert Sheldrake proposed something that quietly disturbed science.

He noticed patterns in nature repeating without clear physical cause.
Animals learning behaviors faster across generations.

Crystals forming more easily once they’d formed before. Skills spreading without direct teaching.
He called it morphic resonance.

The idea was simple: once something happens enough times, it becomes easier for it to happen again — as if reality remembers.

Not through genes. Not through signals. Through a shared field.

Experiments showed learning rates increasing globally. Even when subjects were isolated.

The reaction was immediate.
His work was dismissed.
Publicly mocked.
Labeled heretical.

But it was never disproven.

The unsettling implication remains: nature may not reset each moment — it may carry memory forward.

And if that’s true, nothing you do is ever isolated.

Source: A New Science of Life - Rupert Sheldrake
